</figure>
<div id="A3.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Multimodal Classification</span>:
Finally, we validate the effectiveness of the P2P algorithm on multimodal classification tasks, leveraging the multimodal hateful memes detection dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kiela et al. (2020)</a></cite> as the target, where triggers are inserted into the textual inputs. As shown in Table <a href="#A2.T9" title="Table 9 ‣ Appendix B Baseline Models ‣ P2P: A Poison-to-Poison Remedy for Reliable Backdoor Defense in LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>, it is evident that the ASR consistently exceeds 95% under different types of backdoor attacks. However, with the adoption of the P2P algorithm, the ASR drops significantly, further corroborating the generalizability of our algorithm.</p>
</div>
</section>
</article>
